# Follow the grammar and the teaching TSV below; reply with one valid plasm_program:
#
# Output:
# - Emit only code: either one `plasm_expr`, or one multi-line `plasm_program` with bindings then final roots.
# - Do not emit prose, JSON wrappers, `return`, Markdown fences, or table rows.
# - Prefer bind → narrow/project/transform → few final roots when more than one step is needed.
#
# TSV table semantics:
# - Header is exactly `plasm_expr<TAB>Meaning`; every following row has exactly one tab delimiter.
# - Left cell is either executable syntax or metadata. Right cell is selection guidance only; never copy `Meaning` into output.
# - Executable rows start with an entity surface (`e#`/`Entity`). Metadata-only `p#`/`v#` rows define slots/value domains and are never roots.
# - `Meaning` fragments joined by ` · ` stay inside `Meaning`; they are not operators.
#
# Symbol and fill rules:
# - `e#` = entity surface; `m#` = method/action surface; `p#` = field/capability/query predicate; `r#` = declared relation navigation; `v#` = value-domain metadata only.
# - Relation hops use `.wire` or `.r#` on a row-producing receiver — never bare `p#` after `.` (`p#` in `e#{{…}}` is a filter/param).
# - Entity-ref gloss like `ref:Zone · str · Zone identifier` is type/help text, not syntax.
# - Never write `v#` inside a `plasm_expr`. Use `p#` keys in code and use `v#` rows only to understand allowed values/types.
# - `e#(p#)` identity rows use `p#` for the id slot; substitute the real wire id. `<id>`, `<value>`, `<receiver>`, and `elem` are meta-variables.
# - If a copied row contains `..`, it is an ellipsis for omitted optional keys. Remove `..` or replace it with additional `p#=<value>` assignments before final output.
# - If `Meaning` says `opt: pA, pB`, those keys may be added only as keyed assignments with real values.
# - Projection rows ending `[p#,…]` teach a valid field set. Reuse that suffix only on another expression returning the same entity or list type.
#
# Core surface:
# - Program shape: one `plasm_expr`, or `label = …` bindings then comma-separated final roots (no `return`).
# - Postfix on row producers: `.limit(N)` | `.page_size(N)` | `.sort(field[, dir])` | `.filter{…}` | `.filter(…)` | `.aggregate(specs)` | `.group_by(field, specs)` | `.dedupe(field[, …])` | `.distinct(field[, …])` | `.distinct()` | `.singleton()` | `[fields]` projection.
# - Run gate: pass `pcN` from a prior dry-run to `plasm_run`; MCP `plasm_run` does not accept program continuations.
# - Teaching TSV left cells are executable surfaces (list/get/query/relation/method); copy left cells, substitute placeholders, and compose via bindings.
# - Field projection suffix: `[p#,…]`.
#
# Composition rules:
# - Multi-line programs: one binding per line and final roots last (preferred). Single-line space-separated bindings are coerced; default return is the first binding.
# - Postfix transforms and `[fields]` may chain on any bound node or expression that returns rows.
# - Row text: `report = rows[p#,…] <<TAG` newline template newline `TAG`; Minijinja `rows` is only that source's projected rows.
# - Compose text with `${report.content}` in later heredocs/strings; pass `binding.content` to string params, not the whole row.
# - Do not use `report.content` as a final root or relation receiver. Return `report` if you want the generated text row; continue relations only from row-producing query/relation/projection bindings.
# - Heredoc opener `<<TAG` is followed by newline; the first later line whose trimmed text is `TAG` closes it. Choose a tag not present in the body.
# - Examples: array argument `p#=[e#(<id>), …]`. Quoted strings use only `\"` and `\\` escapes.
# - Worked row-compute program (shape only — substitute `e#` / `p#` from your table below):
# items = e_source
# filtered = items.filter{p_field>=300}  # prefer bind; bare `e_source.filter{…}` list-alls first
# sorted = filtered.sort(p_field, desc)
# limited = sorted.limit(10)
# limited[p_a,p_field]
# - Worked relation + row compute (shape only — copy `.r#`/wire from your table's left column):
# parent = e_parent(p_key=<val>)
# children = parent.rN
# limited = children.limit(5)
# limited[p_a,p_b]
#
# Common pitfalls:
# - Row contract: `{…}` / `~"…"{…}` inputs fetch or filter rows; only `rows:` fields from the teaching row may be used in `[fields]`, `.group_by`, `.sort`, `.dedupe`, or row `.filter` — not `inputs:` / `opt:` filter keys.
# - Fetch vs row filter: `e#{{…}}` filters at HTTP; `binding.filter{{…}}` or `binding.filter(…)` filters materialized rows. Not `rows{{…}}` on a label.
# - Prefer `label = e#` then `label.filter{{…}}`; bare `e#.filter{{…}}` implies list-all first (unbounded read warning).
# - `group_by(key, count=count)`; bare `group_by(key)` means `count=count`. Multi-key: `group_by(k1, k2, n=count)`.
# - Binding `=>`: only `rows => {{ k: _.field }}` (derive) or `rows => e1(…).update(…)` (for_each). Child reads: `labels = issues.r#` or `issues.labels` — not `issues => e2(…)`. Row text: `rows <<TAG`, not `=>`.
# - Homograph / gloss: filter `p#` and relation `.r#`/wire may share a wire name — fanout via `.r#`/wire (`labels = issues.p#` forgiven when the binding name matches). Copy executable left cells, not `Meaning` arrow gloss (`relation e3 → e2`).
# - Fill-ins: never emit `$` from teaching rows; substitute real ids, filter keys, and `e#~"text"` terms before execute.
#

plasm_expr	Meaning
v2	str · Type name (unique key)
v1	int · Numeric type identifier
v3	str · API resource URL
p2	v2 · name
p1	v1 · id
p3	v3 · url
e1[p2,p1,p3]	→ e1 · projection · An elemental type
e1[p2,p1,p3]	→ [e1] · rows: p2,p1,p3
e1(p2)	→ e1
e1.r1	relation e1 → Generation · Generation this type was introduced
e1.r2	relation e1 → MoveDamageClass · Default damage class for this type
e1.r3	relation e1 → [Move] · Moves of this elemental type
e1.r4	relation e1 → [Pokemon] · Pokémon with this type
